The bug is in `org.jfree.data.time.TimePeriodValues.updateBounds(org.jfree.data.time.TimePeriod, int)`.

At [TimePeriodValues.updateBounds(...)](/abs/path/org/jfree/data/time/TimePeriodValues.java:299), the `maxMiddleIndex` update logic reads the middle value from `this.minMiddleIndex` instead of `this.maxMiddleIndex`:

```java
long s = getDataItem(this.minMiddleIndex).getPeriod().getStart().getTime();
long e = getDataItem(this.minMiddleIndex).getPeriod().getEnd().getTime();
long maxMiddle = s + (e - s) / 2;
if (middle > maxMiddle) {
    this.maxMiddleIndex = index;
}
```

That means new items are compared against the current minimum middle, not the current maximum middle. In the failing test, after the third item `(0,50)` is added, `minMiddleIndex` becomes `2` with middle `25`. When the fourth item `(150,200)` with middle `175` is added, the code compares `175 > 25` and incorrectly sets `maxMiddleIndex = 3`, even though item `1` has the true maximum middle `350`.

Expected fix direction: the `maxMiddleIndex` branch should use `this.maxMiddleIndex` when loading the reference period for comparison.

